// @run
// @log 1
// @log 3
// @log 3
// A RECURSIVE type is a CYCLE in the type arena, and `tyToEmitName` renders a
// type STRUCTURALLY — field by field, element by element. Rendering a cycle is an
// infinite string, so on a stock build the recursion never returns and the
// COMPILER ITSELF dies with `wasm trap: call stack exhausted` (a wasm backtrace,
// no diagnostic). Both programs below are single-file and well-typed, and both
// reached it through a DIFFERENT caller:
//
//   1. `Node & {v: i32}` — `canonEmitName`'s intersection arm folds through the
//      checker algebra and renders the fold.
//   2. `type U = Box<Node> | {w: i32}` — `canonEmitTypeNames`' `UnionDecl` arm
//      resolves a generic APPLICATION member to its structural shape
//      (`unionMemberGenAppShape`), which renders the instance.
//
// The guard is `isEquatable`'s: an ANCESTOR stack, and a type already on it has
// no finite emit name — "" , which is what every caller already does with an
// unrenderable type (here: keep the annotation's own nominal spelling, which is
// how every recursive shape the emitter lowers is spelled anyway).
//
// The third leg is the ancestor-vs-seen distinction: `Pair` mentions `S` TWICE as
// SIBLINGS, which is not a cycle. A guard that remembered every index it had ever
// visited would decline the second `b: S` and lose the fold; the push/pop stack
// pops `S` when field `a` finishes, so both fields render.
type Node = { v: i32, next: Node | null }
type Box<T> = { b: T }
type U = Box<Node> | { w: i32 }
type S = { k: i32 }
type Pair = { a: S, b: S }

const n: Node & { v: i32 } = { v: 1, next: null }
print(n.v)

const u: U = { w: 3 }
if u is { w: i32 } { print(u.w) }

const p: Pair & { a: S } = { a: { k: 1 }, b: { k: 2 } }
print(p.a.k + p.b.k)
